{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-94ecba92245fd813","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9014\n3\tepic_count: 7\n4\tcritical_path_length: 5\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9014 (Graduate Llama Stack to General Availability)\n10\t\n11\tStrategy: RHAISTRAT-9014 — Graduate Llama Stack to General Availability (priority: Critical).\n12\t\n13\tTriage (Step 0): not below-threshold — the strategy spans three components\n14\t(Llama Stack, TrustyAI, Dashboard) owned by three distinct teams and carries a\n15\tP0–P2 HLR range; nor docs-only — Llama Stack and Dashboard require code\n16\tchanges. Full decomposition follows.\n17\t\n18\tThe strategy's central unknown (its only open question) is that the GA scope\n19\titself is unknown until a systematic GA-readiness assessment lands: TP\n20\tfeedback is scattered across support tickets and no GA-readiness assessment\n21\thas been done. That unknown changes what the downstream stability and\n22\thardening epics do, so it becomes the Investigation epic below (Step 3\n23\tdecision rule: YES → Investigation).\n24\t\n25\t## Epic List\n26\t\n27\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n28\t|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n29\t| E001 | GA-readiness assessment for Llama Stack | Investigation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n30\t| E002 | Llama Stack API stability guarantee and tested upgrade path | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n31\t| E003 | Llama Stack production support posture | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n32\t| E004 | Promote TrustyAI guardrails integration to supported in GA distributions | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P1 |\n33\t| E005 | TrustyAI-side validation of the Llama Stack guardrails integration | Implementation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P1 |\n34\t| E006 | Enable Llama Stack management by default in the Dashboard | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n35\t| E007 | Llama Stack GA documentation uplift | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI GenAI | Documentation | P0 |\n36\t\n37\tTeams are inferred from `.context/architecture-context/` owning-team fields\n38\t(the strategy has no Impacted Teams table — see Health Warnings).\n39\t\n40\t## Dependency DAG\n41\t\n42\t```mermaid\n43\tgraph TD\n44\t    E001[\"E001 GA-readiness assessment (Inv)\"] --> E002[\"E002 API stability + upgrade path\"]\n45\t    E001 --> E003[\"E003 Production support posture\"]\n46\t    E002 --> E004[\"E004 Guardrails integration promotion\"]\n47\t    E004 --> E005[\"E005 TrustyAI validation\"]\n48\t    E002 --> E007[\"E007 GA docs uplift\"]\n49\t    E003 --> E007\n50\t    E004 --> E007\n51\t    E005 --> E007\n52\t    E006[\"E006 Dashboard default enablement\"] --> E007\n53\t```\n54\t\n55\tCritical path: E001 → E002 → E004 → E005 → E007 (length 5).\n56\t\n57\t## DAG Justification\n58\t\n59\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n60\t|------|------|-----------|\n61\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gate) | The assessment inventories API stability and upgrade-path gaps and produces the definitive GA scope; its outcome changes the size and content of the stability work. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n62\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gate) | The assessment's scale-limit and error-handling findings determine SLO targets and the supported configuration matrix content. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n63\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 12/13 (framework → consumer) | E002 delivers the stable, versioned published API surface; the guardrails integration is made *supported* against that surface. Building the supported integration before the API surface is stabilized would force a second validation pass. |\n64\t| E004 → E005 | Step 2 exception (cross-team validation) | The TrustyAI team's validation epic validates the promoted integration; the promotion must exist before it can be validated. |\n65\t| E002 → E007 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Docs describe what was built. |\n66\t| E003 → E007 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n67\t| E004 → E007 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n68\t| E005 → E007 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n69\t| E006 → E007 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n70\t\n71\tNon-edges (parallel work):\n72\t\n73\t- **E002 ∥ E003**: same component and team, but different logical changes\n74\t  (engineering stability work vs. support-posture authoring); both gated only\n75\t  by the assessment. No edge between them (Rule 15).\n76\t- **E006 parallel to everything except E007** (Rule 14): the Dashboard lives in\n77\t  a different repo, reads/writes Llama Stack CRs via the Kubernetes API (per\n78\t  architecture context, the Dashboard owns no data plane and calls no private\n79\t  component APIs), and builds against no Llama Stack artifact. Its coupling to\n80\t  GA timing is release coordination, noted as a risk on the epic — not a DAG\n81\t  dependency.\n82\t- **No konflux/repo/license onboarding chain**: all three components already\n83\t  exist in the platform with architecture-context files and are Konflux\n84\t  onboarded (Rules 20/25 do not apply).\n85\t\n86\tRule 19 (safety-critical strategy) was evaluated and does not trigger: the\n87\tstrategy's core is GA graduation of an existing Tech Preview component, not\n88\tthe construction of new guardrail/sandbox/RBAC capability. The TrustyAI\n89\tguardrails integration already ships experimentally, TrustyAI itself is\n90\tStable (GA), and the integration's correctness is covered by the dedicated\n91\tTrustyAI-team validation epic (E005).\n92\t\n93\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n94\t\n95\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abbrev.) | Epic(s) | Notes |\n96\t|-----|----------|----------------------|---------|-------|\n97\t| HLR-1 | P0 | GA-readiness assessment inventories gaps, produces definitive GA scope | E001 | The assessment IS the Investigation epic; its deliverable is the gap inventory + GA scope. |\n98\t| HLR-2 | P0 | API stability guarantee: versioned APIs, deprecation policy, tested upgrade path from last two TP releases | E002 | Gated by E001 (scope validated by assessment). |\n99\t| HLR-3 | P0 | Production support posture: SLO targets, backport policy, supported configuration matrix | E003 | Gated by E001 (scale-limit findings feed SLOs and config matrix). |\n100\t| HLR-4 | P1 | TrustyAI guardrails a supported, documented integration for GA distributions | E004, E005 | Split across teams per Step 2: the active enablement change is on the Llama Stack distribution (E004, RHOAI GenAI); TrustyAI is the passive component validated by a different team, which per the Step 2 exception generates the separate TrustyAI-team validation epic (E005). Both epics carry the P1 priority of HLR-4. |\n101\t| HLR-5 | P1 | Dashboard enables Llama Stack management by default for entitled clusters | E006 | Independent of the assessment — the Dashboard gap areas (flag default, entitlement) are fully specified by the HLR. |\n102\t| HLR-6 | P2 | Documentation uplifted to GA standards incl. TP migration notes | E007 | `docs-authoring` priority exception: strategy frontmatter priority is Critical → epic priority P0. This priority does NOT propagate upstream to E002–E006 (Rule 11 edges exempt from priority inheritance). |\n103\t\n104\tAll six HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n105\t\n106\t## Health Warnings\n107\t\n108\t- **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted\n109\t  Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Acceptance Criteria\n110\t  (Given/When/Then) section, no Non-Functional Requirements, and no Technical\n111\t  Approach / Staff Engineer Input. Teams were inferred from\n112\t  `.context/architecture-context/` owning-team fields; acceptance criteria were\n113\t  derived from the HLRs. Human verification of team assignments recommended.\n114\t- **GA scope is an estimate until E001 lands** (the strategy's own open\n115\t  question): E002 and E003 are scoped from the HLR text; the assessment may\n116\t  resize them or surface additional remediation work (handled via the\n117\t  `rewrite` gate and follow-up epics, not by inflating these epics).\n118\t- **Priority note, not an inversion**: E007 is P0 via the docs-authoring\n119\t  exception (strategy priority Critical) while covering the P2 HLR-6. This is\n120\t  per-rule, recorded here because it reads as an inversion at a glance.\n121\t- **Assumption on HLR-4 boundary**: the active code change for the TrustyAI\n122\t  guardrails integration is assumed to be on the Llama Stack distribution side\n123\t  (removing the experimental flag, distribution config, validation); TrustyAI\n124\t  itself is Stable and needs no code change for the integration to be\n125\t  supported. If assessment or refinement shows TrustyAI-side code changes are\n126\t  needed, E005 grows from validation to implementation.\n127\t\n128\t## Component Mapping Notes\n129\t\n130\t- All epic `component` values are verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`.\n131\t- E004's work spans Llama Stack (distribution enablement) and TrustyAI (the\n132\t  integrated component); assigned the single most-relevant canonical name\n133\t  `Llama Stack` since the change lands in the Llama Stack distribution.\n134\t- E007 uses canonical component `Documentation` (docs content work) owned by\n135\t  the RHOAI GenAI team, which owns the Llama Stack subject matter; its scope\n136\t  covers Llama Stack GA documentation.\n137\t\n138\t## Tiered Delivery\n139\t\n140\tNot applicable — no external dependency with uncertain timing. TrustyAI is an\n141\tin-platform, Stable (GA) component, so HLR-4 involves no upstream gating.\n142\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8c6d160a01e76188","agent_id":"a5f8ac71d1d2d3442","session_id":"85972203-1fc3-4cec-8474-9537ba830cbb","uuid":"674f7965-6ac1-4c1a-9e0a-dc7c61b5e630","timestamp":"2026-10-26T11:28:04.653Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-94ecba92245fd813","permission_decision":{"decisio